## Report
Validated the regression at commit 6b821bc819a9547b3f38564c2c5739975decb845. Code inspection of the targeted diff shows `validate_static_record_token_and_expiry(...)` was moved from before to after signature comparison in both affected paths: presigned SigV4 now returns `AuthError::SignatureMismatch` at `crates/auth/src/request.rs:543-547` before calling the helper at `request.rs:548-552`; the helper checks credential expiry and returns `ExpiredToken` at `request.rs:605-613`. POST SigV4 has the same ordering at `crates/auth/src/post.rs:120-134`. The still-present upstream unit test `request::tests::presigned_expired_token` at `request.rs:2031-2055` expects `ExpiredToken` for an expired record with a bad signature, and it now panics: `assertion failed: matches!(err, AuthError::ExpiredToken)`. I then added a targeted integration PoC using the public API. It confirmed both affected paths return `SignatureMismatch` for expired credentials with bad signatures: `PRESIGNED_EXPIRED_BAD_SIGNATURE_ERROR=SignatureMismatch` and `POST_EXPIRED_BAD_SIGNATURE_ERROR=SignatureMismatch`. Control cases using valid signatures still return `ExpiredToken`, confirming this is an error-precedence regression rather than an authentication bypass. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container. LLDB was used non-interactively and stopped in `authenticate_presigned` at `request.rs:545`, the signature comparison, before the expiry helper breakpoint at `request.rs:610`, corroborating the execution order.

